    label: "The Day That Was",
    kicker: "Daily Wrap",
    headline: "Nifty closes above 25,000 for the first time since February as ICICI Bank and HDFC Bank carry the Sensex 1,718 points higher \u2014 even as 2,141 stocks fall and both mid- and small-caps end in the red",
    summary: "The rally came from exactly one place: the two largest private banks. ICICI Bank rose 4.75% to Rs 1,571.20 and HDFC Bank 3.72% to Rs 788.25, and between them they carried the Sensex 1,718.01 points, or 2.17%, to 81,028.02 and the Nifty 493.75 points, or 2.01%, to 25,030.35 \u2014 both closing at their session highs, and the Nifty above 25,000 for the first time since 27 February. The fuel was Thursday's foreign flow: FIIs bought Rs 14,975 crore of Indian equities on 5 November, the largest single-day purchase in nearly six years, and the rupee strengthened to 93.90 against 94.41 a dollar, a six-month high, while Brent for December settled 0.71% lower at $97.59. Everything else was noise by comparison. Nifty Private Bank rose 3.22%, Financial Services 2.57% and Bank 2.27% to 59,086.00; Consumer Durables added 1.70%, Pharma 1.21%, Infra 0.77%, Auto 0.76%, FMCG 0.67% and Metal 0.66%, with IT up only 0.43% to 32,977.90 despite the Nasdaq's 5.52% overnight crash. That last number is the surprise: Wall Street's worst session in more than a year \u2014 Nvidia -9%, Qualcomm -9%, Micron -17.5%, the S&P 500 -4.36% \u2014 barely touched Indian IT, because the same foreign money that was leaving AI infrastructure was arriving in Indian financials. What it did hit was the domestic capex trade. Media fell 1.52%, Defence 0.92% and Energy 0.05%, and BSE Capital Goods lost 1.36% as Thermax collapsed 17.27% on a Q2 miss, Astral 9.68% on a margin collapse, Apar Industries 9.65% and CG Power 4.43%. Breadth was the session's honest tell: 1,438 NSE stocks rose and 2,141 fell, with 127 unchanged. The Nifty Midcap 150 ended 0.09% lower, the Smallcap 250 0.85% lower and the Nifty 500 only 1.00% higher against the Nifty's 2.01%. India VIX rose 13.35% to 17.66 while the index rose 2%, which is protection being bought into the Diwali week rather than confidence. The next session is not Monday: NSE and BSE hold the Muhurat trading session for Diwali Laxmi Pujan on Sunday, 8 November, from 2:20 pm to 3:30 pm, opening Samvat 2083.",
    stats: ["Nifty 2.01%", "Sensex 2.17%"],
